# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measuring
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

class CpuSampler:
    """Per-CPU busy fraction from /proc/stat, as deltas between calls."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.prev = {}

    def sample(self):
        loads = {}
        with open("/proc/stat") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                if not line.startswith("cpu") or line.startswith("cpu "):
                    continue
                parts = line.split()
                cpu = int(parts[0][3:])
                vals = [int(v) for v in parts[1:]]
                idle = vals[3] + (vals[4] if len(vals) > 4 else 0)
                total = sum(vals[:8])  # guest time is already in user
                prev = self.prev.get(cpu)
                self.prev[cpu] = (total, idle)
                if prev and total > prev[0]:
                    busy = 1.0 - (idle - prev[1]) / (total - prev[0])
                    loads[cpu] = min(1.0, max(0.0, busy))
        return loads


class GroupSampler:
    """CPUs' worth of time used per cgroup, from cpu.stat usage_usec."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.prev = {}

    @staticmethod
    def _usage(path):
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(path, "cpu.stat")) as fh:
                for line in fh:
                    if line.startswith("usage_usec "):
                        return int(line.split()[1])
        except (OSError, TypeError):
            pass
        return None

    def sample(self, paths):
        """paths: {name: cgroup dir or None}. Returns {name: cpus used}."""
        now = GLib.get_monotonic_time()
        out = {}
        for name, path in paths.items():
            usage = self._usage(path)
            prev = self.prev.get(name)
            self.prev[name] = (path, now, usage)
            if usage is None or not prev or prev[0] != path or prev[2] is None:
                out[name] = 0.0
                continue
            dt = now - prev[1]
            out[name] = max(0.0, (usage - prev[2]) / dt) if dt > 0 else 0.0
        return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The valley: what lives there, and the rules it lives by
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

class World:
    """The valley around the fort, run headless: no drawing, no pixels.

    The world's rules. Time steps in whole-second ticks, and every choice,
    catch and drain happens in one; nothing is scripted, each actor reacts
    to what it needs and what is near it, and everything it does costs
    time. The currencies are time, body (a beaver's condition) and stores
    (sticks), drawn from finite resources that regrow.

    Seasons. A year is an hour of world time, fifteen minutes a season,
    spring first; each blends into the next over its last minute. The
    clock runs whether the fort is up or down. Winter's cold keeps the
    beavers in their lodges; spring's high water breaks a little off
    every dam and lodge.

    Trees. A fixed set of slots on each flank, in the woods back from the
    river and along its banks. A felled tree leaves a stump (gnawed to a
    point by a beaver, cut flat by an axe), which grows back as a sapling
    and is a full tree again after REGROW_S.

    Beavers. Two colonies, one on each flank's stretch of river, each
    with its own dam site. A beaver's body is its condition: it drains
    all the time and faster in winter, more so without a dam's pond to
    keep a larder in; at none it starves. It feeds on bark along its own
    banks (full trees feed best, saplings less, the pond's weed barely),
    and it works when it has the strength and its colony needs sticks:
    fells a bank tree into a pile of sticks, then carries them one at a
    time to the dam, and once the dam is half up, to the lodge. Winter
    keeps them all inside. Kits come at mid-spring from the colony's
    surplus condition (each costs its mother KIT_COST), only as far as
    the lodge has room; no lodge, no kits. Rails: a colony left empty
    takes a pair of the other's young if it has plenty; a valley with
    no beavers at all for a season gets a pair from upstream.

    Trappers. Each keeps to one flank and one cabin plot there, and his
    body is his health and how fed he is. Whenever he is free he weighs
    what he needs, on his own, and does the most pressing thing; every
    choice costs the walk there and the work:
    - trap: set a trap at one of his flank's sites (shared: a trap set is
      its setter's until he collects it, or lifts it when the bait has
      gone stale), worth more the more beavers
      are about and the hungrier he is, and nothing under the ice. A set
      trap catches with a chance that grows with the beavers out of
      their lodge; he collects the catch and stashes it at his cabin for
      a pelt and MEAT_PER_BEAVER meals;
    - fire: gather wood (or burn a log, quicker), cook a meal and eat,
      more pressing the hungrier he is; without meat he cannot;
    - chop: fell the nearest standing tree of his flank, woods before
      banks, for logs to build with and burn: as many as the cabin still
      needs plus WINTER_LOGS, more urgent as winter nears;
    - build: lay one log on his cabin, likewise more urgent toward
      winter;
    - shelter: sit out the cold inside his cabin, which keeps out the
      share of it that its walls are built;
    - rest.
    Hunger drains his fed-ness; at none, his health drains. Cold drains
    his health whenever he is outside in winter, less at a fire, and
    being fed and warm heals it. At no health he dies, of whichever was
    taking him; a grave marks the spot and weathers away. Time spent
    trapping in autumn is time not spent on the cabin.
    When a trapper dies, the nearest survivor (his own flank first, then
    whoever has least) takes his pelts, meat and logs, and moves into
    his cabin if it is further along than his own; with none left, the
    stores are lost and the cabin stands for the next arrival. A cabin
    with nobody in it loses a log a season. More trappers means the
    sites, the trees and the beavers go further around; fewer, the
    more each one gets. Newcomers arrive in spring, more the more
    beavers the valley holds (ARRIVE_K), into the free plot furthest
    along. Through summer and autumn a lone drifter may come the same
    way, a chance each tick that grows with the square of the plots
    standing free and with the beavers (DRIFT_K), and fades as winter
    nears; none travel in the cold. So a death is felt for a while and
    then filled, sooner the emptier the valley. Rails: a spring with
    nobody left brings one regardless; never more than TRAPPERS_MAX.

    Bears. Each tick gives them their turn before anyone else's. A bear
    keeps to one flank, and its body is its energy and its health. It
    comes in from the flank's edge, at most one a flank, more often the
    more the flank offers it: beavers out, beavers in traps, meat in the
    caches and men about, all counting for more in the seasons the river
    fishes well; a valley with none of these brings none. Energy drains
    all the time it is afoot and a little while it sleeps; at none it
    starves. Whenever it is free it weighs what each errand would gain it
    a minute, walk and work together, and does the best:
    - fish at a riffle, poor in spring, good in summer, best in autumn,
      nothing under the ice;
    - hunt: wait at a bank tree for a beaver to come within reach, a
      better chance the more are out;
    - steal: take a beaver from a trap that has caught one;
    - raid: rob a cache of meals, only while its owner is well away;
    - stalk a man: worth more the hungrier it is, less the further it
      has to charge, and against it is its dread of him, which hunger
      wears away and each other man within NEAR_D of him multiplies, so
      a lone man is the one it goes for, and only a hungry bear goes
      for any, and none hurt past the point where it would break off
      a fight (below);
    - wander.
    Its belly is the cap on all of these: a full bear does not eat, it
    wanders. Winter it sleeps in a den on its flank, from the first cold
    to the thaw, so it wakes hungry in spring; one with little left and
    nothing on its flank worth staying for walks out the way it came.
    Rails: never more than BEARS_MAX.
    A stalked man may see it coming, the likelier the closer
    it is; he is watchful (VIGILANCE) when he is fed and not bent over
    his work, and once he has seen it he stands his ground. If he moves
    off before it reaches him, it lets him go.
    The fight. If he saw it coming he gets one shot as it closes, better
    aimed (likelier to hit, and harder) the longer he had from raising
    his rifle (REACT_S) to the moment it reached him, full aim taking
    AIM_S; there is no reloading. Then it is claws against his knife,
    an exchange every EXCHANGE_S: the bear swipes the man in front of
    it and every man at it stabs, each blow's chance scaled by the
    striker's health and fed-ness or energy. The shot carries HEAR_D
    (the struggle alone a third of that): every other man of the flank
    within it runs to help, fires once as he arrives, and joins in. A
    man at no health dies of the bear. A bear hurt below FLEE_AT breaks
    off and runs for its den; the hungrier, the longer it presses on. A
    bear that leaves no man standing eats the one it killed. Nobody
    pins the outcome: it falls out of noticing, aim, help and the
    exchanges.
    After. A slain bear is skinned where it fell: its hide, worth
    HIDE_PELTS pelts, goes to the man who did it most harm (it hangs
    stretched at his cabin for a season), and its meat is shared among
    the men there. A man the bear kills leaves his goods and cabin like
    any other, so the fewer men there are the more each one has; and
    the men of his flank stay watchful (ALERT_VIG) for ALERT_S, idling
    at a neighbour's cabin rather than their own, which puts them in
    company. Wounds heal only as the rest of him does, fed and warm, so
    a man mauled in autumn goes into the winter with less to lose. A
    bear that got away heals slowly while it is well fed.
    """

